Build profile pkg.rustc.upstream-bin
====================================

Not part of Debian or Proxmox: carried by the s390x port of Proxmox VE
(https://github.com/cyberdotgent/rustc, branch s390x/upstream-bin).

Proxmox's Rust pipeline (debcargo, dh-cargo, librust-*-dev, offline builds
against /usr/share/cargo/registry) needs the Debian toolchain packages (rustc,
cargo, libstd-rust-dev, ...) installed as dpkg-owned files in Debian paths.
For architectures where no such packages exist at the required version, this
profile repackages the official upstream binary release instead of building
from source:

  rust-<version>-<rust-triple>.tar.xz  (static.rust-lang.org/dist, the same
                                         bits rustup installs)
  rust-std-<version>-wasm32-{unknown-unknown,wasip1,wasip2}.tar.xz
                                        (wasm std for libstd-rust-dev-wasm32;
                                         not with the nowasm profile)

Without the profile, the package builds exactly as before.

Usage:

  export DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=pkg.rustc.upstream-bin
  # optional: where the tarball + .asc live (default debian/upstream-bin/dl)
  export UPSTREAM_BIN_DIR=/path/to/cache
  debian/rules upstream-bin-fetch      # needs network; skips existing files
  dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

What the profile does:

  * verifies each tarball's sha256 against debian/upstream-bin/SHA256SUMS
    (pinned here; cross-checked against the GPG-signed
    channel-rust-<version>.toml when added) and its detached GPG signature
    against the Rust release key in debian/upstream/signing-key.asc (gpgv;
    sqv rejects the key's SHA-1 binding signatures)
  * runs the upstream install.sh (components rustc, cargo, rust-std,
    rustfmt, clippy, rust-analyzer) into a staging tree, smoke-tests it
    (rustc/cargo versions, hello world, cargo test, a wasm32 cdylib), then applies the same
    post-install layout as the source build (librustc_driver etc. in
    /usr/lib/<multiarch>, no libstd dylib) and the normal *.install files,
    so Debian's glue (/usr/share/cargo/bin/cargo, /usr/share/cargo/scripts,
    /usr/share/rustc/architecture.mk) is included
  * builds only rustc, cargo, libstd-rust-<ver>, libstd-rust-dev, rustfmt,
    rust-clippy, rust-analyzer, libstd-rust-dev-wasm32 (arch all) and
    rust-llvm (no docs, rust-src, bpf std, rust-gdb, rust-lldb, rust-all)
  * rust-llvm: Debian's package only has symlinks to lld-21/clang-21/llvm-21,
    which aren't packaged for every architecture (trixie has none). Instead it
    carries the upstream release's rust-lld, gcc-ld/ wrappers,
    wasm-component-ld and rust-objcopy in /usr/lib/rustlib/<triple>/bin
    (built from the same LLVM as rustc) and /usr/bin/rust-lld -> that
    rust-lld (wasm builds use linker=rust-lld); its Depends on the LLVM 21
    packages is dropped (no rust-clang/rust-llvm-dwp/llvm-cov links)
  * does not strip/dwz the upstream binaries (shipped bit-identical)
  * moves the cargo binary to /usr/lib/cargo/bin/cargo; /usr/bin/cargo is a
    small wrapper (debian/upstream-bin/cargo-wrapper) that selects cargo's
    "dev" channel like Debian's patch d-0012-cargo-always-return-dev-channel,
    which dh-cargo needs (-Zavoid-dev-deps)
  * known behaviour difference left alone: Debian's rustc patch
    d-rustc-add-soname (DT_SONAME on dylib/cdylib outputs) is not in the
    upstream binary
  * versions the binary packages <upstream>+upstream-<UPSTREAM_BIN_REVISION>
    (default 0proxport3), e.g. 1.94.1+upstream-0proxport3: sorts above the
    source-built 1.94.1+dfsg1-*, below 1.94.1++ and the next release

To move to a new upstream version, rebase onto the new packaging, fetch the
new tarball, verify it (gpgv + signed channel manifest) and update
SHA256SUMS.
